# Harmony From First Principles

All sound can be expressed as sums of sine waves
Two sine waves are perceived as:
- Consonant if almost exactly the same frequency
- Dissonant if near each other, with a peak in dissonance at around 50 cents
- Less dissonant if far away from each other
Surprisingly, just intervals don't matter for pure sine waves
Those patterns in music only show up once overtones from real instruments are involved

Use PyTorch to generate random weights
Treat these weights as inputs to an instrument (a fundamental and overtones)
Calculate loss on the resulting sound
Optimize the weights

## Future Plans

### Code Robustness and Alternate Musical Systems

12-tone equal temperament, 12 tones, and in fact having equal divisions at all is not universal in music
We just happen to use 12-TET and A4=440Hz because having all MIDI stuff work seamlessly is nice

Since the dissonance matrix is defined for all "keys" against all other "keys", "keys" can support arbitrary frequencies
Pythagorean, 1/4 comma meantone, 1/3 comma meantone, etc.
We can even get rid of 12 divisions of the octave
24-EDO, 31-EDO, 19-EDO, etc.
Or even the octave
alpha, beta, gamma, etc.

We still need to have discrete keys for optimization though
Bends and vibrato are impossible

For multiple band members below:
We also need a more robust way to map multiple weight[key, beat] to each other, where beat may be of different lengths
All the different weights also need to be exported to one audio

### Multiple Band Members

Right now it's just 🎹 going ham on the keyboard. The "beats" as named in the code are really chords or bars
- Add 🎸 , a monophonic (not chords) member that does guitar things every beat
- Add 🥁 , which honestly I'm not sure how to turn into an optimization problem. It will probably just do kicks on 1 and 3 and snares on 2 and 4 and have a button to toggle on or off
- Add 🍜 (there is no bass emoji), a monophonic member that, uh, what do basses do again? I think they're down to earth or something

Monophonic members when playing audio will only play the strongest weight

These members will probably need new "instrument" parameters. The current default instrument is very much a synth and does not behave like any real instrument
Attack, decay, sustain, and release would be nice
Per-harmonic ADSR would be even better but that would make a note's dissonance with other notes change over time, so the dissonance matrix might have to use an average value

At the same time, probably add losses for instrument range and interval jumps

### Gradio

Gradio is the worst ML-demo-to-web-page framework, except for all others we've tried
Uses Python, allows the same codebase to do both PyTorch and Gradio stuff, and can be deployed easily
TODO: test how slow PyTorch on 2 vCPUs on the free HuggingFace space is

### User Input

Ties into multiple band members and a Gradio interface
What if the user had yet another piano roll that they could place notes on? Those notes would be fixed and apply dissonance pressure on the random noise
Sort of like partial denoising in image-to-image

We could also impose Western diatonic music by choosing certain pitch classes to have higher loss

## After Kimi K2.5's Work

Wow, so after calling you to implement the rest of the Future Plans to see what you might come up with, you ended up with a Gradio UI that outputs music.
Pretty impressive

Unfortunately there's some bugs:
- The piano dies after 20 optimization steps, a behaviour that did not exist in my initial main.py and main_optimized.py. You can see 100_steps_2e-2_lr.png (default 100 steps, 0.02 lr) is dead but 10_steps_1e-2_lr.png (10 steps, 0.01 lr) has some activity
- - This is a canon event. Refer to https://www.sekaipedia.org/wiki/Tenma_Saki for more information

And there's bigger architecture issues:
- The four members (🎸🎹🥁🍜) are hardcoded into the UI. I expected a more flexible interface where you can add an instrument from a predefined role (piano, guitar, etc.) or create your own
- There seems to be no way to change the number of measures in the piece of music. It's always 4 chords. Also, 🎸 and 🍜 only play whole notes, instead of cooler things like 8th, 12th, and 16th notes that the user can set
- I cannot for the life of me understand how the constraints tab works. I was expecting a grid where I could "paint" the weights of each band member and my painted pixels would have grad set to 0

All in all, good job, but I'm gonna have to rebuild this from the ground up over a week or two
AI in February 2026 is good but it's not yet at "satisfying all written and unwritten expectations by typing one prompt into Roo Code and coming back the next morning"
I think large parts of your code can be used as a reference though

## v3

v1 (2026-02-02) contains files from the initial implementation: solo synth-like member using ReLU
v2 (2026-02-05) contains Kimi K2.5's implementation
Anything outside those two folders is work for v3

### 2026-02-07
- main.py: does a simple test that creates a song with a piano, guitar, bass, and synth and renders the audio. Obviously it is random noise and produces awful clipping
- song.py: Song
- members.py: Member, PolyphonicMember, MonophonicMember
- - Defaults: piano, guitar, bass, synth
- instruments.py: ADSR, Instrument
- - Defaults: piano, guitar, bass, synth
- tuning_systems.py: TuningSystem
- - Defaults: 12-TET
- audio_service.py: AudioService
- color_service.py: ColorService

### 2026-02-08
- Kimi K2.5 implemented LossHandler and OptimHandler nicely
- main.py:
- - Now supports any number of members and plots their activations (argmax for MonophonicMember). You still need to edit the code though
- - Now has a non-interactive mode by doing `python main.py 40 60 80 100` etc. Good for AI models in Roo Code to do experiments
- - - You know you can use `read_file` on image paths in the output folder right?
- Quietness and muddyness losses have been replaced with an ideal amplitude and amplitude loss while I think of a better loss function
- - I want the optimizer to find chords with a few strong notes. You know, like what a keyboard member would play

### 2026-02-09
- Kimi K2.5 fixed the weight painting bugs (the correct PyTorch pattern is to use torch.where to combine weights and painted during the forward pass)
- Band members each have a velocity now. Finally, the synth won't be overpowering everything

### 2026-02-11
- A tanh compressor (based on v2) has been added to AudioService
- Pythagorean and meantone tuning systems, and a tuning demo GUI for comparison
- EDO systems and non-octave systems (not comparable in the demo since they're not compatible with MIDI's 128 notes)

### 2026-02-12
- A whole bunch of new instruments

### 2026-02-13
- VoiceInstrument: choir oohs and aahs, accepts formants, actual harmonics change depending on fundamental frequency (required updating loss_handler)
- PercussionInstrument: currently only supports 35 (Acoustic Bass Drum) and 38 (Acoustic Snare)
- - Patch notes: added Mochizuki Honami

Current issues:
- Subjective issues with solutions the optimizer finds (not sure if these are solvable)
- - Chords and progressions are, uh, interesting
- - Optimum at ~300 steps for guitar and bass is to collapse to the midpoints of their range: E4 and C3 respectively
- - Monophonic instruments jump around in a "band" surrounding the midpoint of their range. They don't go up and down melodically
